    d.h1('4. The two definitions, and four corrections')
    d.h2('4.1 [1A] The octonions')
    d.p('The octonions $\\mathbb{O}$ form an 8-dimensional, non-associative, non-commutative normed division algebra over '
        '$\\mathbb{R}$, with basis $e_0=1,e_1,\\dots,e_7$:')
    d.align([r'x&=\textstyle\sum_{A=0}^{7}x_A e_A,\qquad e_i^2=-1\ (i=1,\dots,7),\qquad e_ie_j=-e_je_i\ (i\neq j),',
             r'\bar x&=x_0-\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{7}x_ie_i,\qquad \overline{xy}=\bar y\,\bar x,\qquad \mathrm{Re}(x)=\tfrac12(x+\bar x)=x_0,',
             r'N(x)&=x\bar x=\bar x x=x_0^2+x_1^2+\dots+x_7^2,\qquad N(xy)=N(x)N(y),\qquad x^{-1}=\bar x/N(x),',
             r'x(xy)&=(xx)y,\qquad (yx)x=y(xx)\qquad\text{(alternativity; power-associativity follows).}'])
    d.p('Products of distinct imaginary units follow the oriented lines of the Fano plane, each line spanning a quaternion '
        'subalgebra. Cayley–Dickson: $\\mathbb{O}=\\mathbb{H}\\oplus\\mathbb{H}$ as pairs of quaternions.')
    d.h2('4.2 [1B] The Clifford algebra of an 8-dimensional space')
    d.align([r'Cl(V,Q)&=\mathcal T(V)/\mathcal I_Q(V),\qquad \mathcal I_Q(V)=\big\langle v\otimes v-Q(v)1\big\rangle,',
             r'v^2&=Q(v)\,1,\qquad vw+wv=2B(v,w)\,1,\qquad B(v,w)=\tfrac12\big(Q(v+w)-Q(v)-Q(w)\big),',
             r'e_A^2&=Q(e_A),\qquad e_Ae_B=-e_Be_A\ (A\neq B),\qquad \dim Cl(V,Q)=2^8=\textstyle\sum_{k=0}^{8}\binom8k=256,',
             r'Cl_8(\mathbb C)&\cong\mathbb C(16).'])
    d.h2('4.3 Corrections (each verified in 7+1.nb)')
    d.ol(['**Cayley–Dickson formula.** The second formula of [1A], $(a,b)(c,d)=(ac-\\bar d b,\\ \\bar a d+cb)$, does not '
          'define the octonions: it is not alternative and its norm is not multiplicative. Counterexample: $x=(1,1)=e_0+e_4$, '
          '$y=(i,0)=e_1$ give $x^2=(0,2)$, $x^2y=(0,2i)$, but $xy=(i,i)$ and $x(xy)=(2i,2i)$. A correct formula of the same '
          'shape is $(a,b)(c,d)=(ac-d\\bar b,\\ \\bar a d+cb)$ (J. C. Baez, *The Octonions*, Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 39 (2002), '
          'section 2.2); the octonions of this document satisfy it exactly with $x=a+\\ell b$, $\\ell=e_4$. The first formula '
          'of [1A] contains a stray symbol "x" and cannot be read unambiguously.',
          '**Grade 4.** $\\binom84=70$, not 72: $1+8+28+56+70+56+28+8+1=256$.',
          '**Classification.** With $v^2=Q(v)$ and $p$ = number of generators squaring to $+1$, the correct table is the one '
          'below. $\\mathbb C(16)$ can never occur: its real dimension is $2\\cdot16^2=512$, while every $Cl(p,q)$ with $p+q=8$ '
          'has real dimension $2^8=256$. Several "Metric Signs" entries of [1B] were also mistyped.',
          '**Split octonions are not a division algebra.** In the split-octonion table of 4+4.nb, '
          '$(\\varepsilon_1+\\varepsilon_5)(\\varepsilon_1-\\varepsilon_5)=\\varepsilon_1-\\varepsilon_5^2=0$ although both factors are '
          'nonzero. They form a composition algebra with an indefinite norm of signature (4,4).'])
    cls = [('8,0', '+,+,+,+,+,+,+,+', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', 'agrees'),
           ('7,1', '+,+,+,+,+,+,+,−', '$\\mathbb H(8)$', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', 'wrong in [1B]'),
           ('6,2', '+,+,+,+,+,+,−,−', '$\\mathbb H(8)$', '$\\mathbb H(8)$', 'agrees (signs mistyped)'),
           ('5,3', '+,+,+,+,+,−,−,−', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', '$\\mathbb C(16)$', 'wrong in [1B]'),
           ('4,4', '+,+,+,+,−,−,−,−', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', 'agrees (signs mistyped)'),
           ('3,5', '+,+,+,−,−,−,−,−', '$\\mathbb H(8)$', '$\\mathbb C(16)$', 'wrong in [1B]'),
           ('2,6', '+,+,−,−,−,−,−,−', '$\\mathbb H(8)$', '$\\mathbb H(8)$', 'agrees (signs mistyped)'),
           ('1,7', '+,−,−,−,−,−,−,−', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', 'agrees'),
           ('0,8', '−,−,−,−,−,−,−,−', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', '$\\mathbb R(16)$', 'agrees')]
    d.table(['$Cl(p,q)$', 'squares of the generators', 'correct', 'claimed in [1B]', 'status'],
            [['$Cl(%s)$' % c[0], c[1], c[2], c[3], c[4]] for c in cls], widths=[0.12, 0.26, 0.14, 0.18, 0.24])
    d.p('**Consequence for this project.** With $v^2=Q(v)$ and the sign convention of 4+4.nb ($Q=\\eta^{(7,1)}$) the Clifford '
        'algebra of 7+1 spacetime is $Cl(7,1)\\cong\\mathbb H(8)$: no real 16×16 matrices exist (section 7.4). With the opposite '
        'overall sign ($Q=\\eta^{(1,7)}$, the octonions\' own form $\\mathrm{Re}(xx)$) it is $Cl(1,7)\\cong\\mathbb R(16)$, '
        'realized by the real matrices $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$ built from 4+4.nb (section 7.2). Both describe the same spacetime: '
        'one time and seven space dimensions.')

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    d.h1('5. Recap: the 4+4 construction, reproduced exactly from 4+4.nb')
    d.h2('5.1 The 8×8 matrices $\\sigma$, $\\zeta_A$, $\\tau_A$, $\\bar\\tau_A$')
    d.p('With $\\epsilon_{hpq4}$ the sign of the permutation $(h,p,q,4)$ (Mathematica `Signature[{h, p, q, 4}]`), 4+4.nb defines '
        'the self-dual and anti-self-dual antisymmetric 4×4 matrices')
    d.eq(r'(s_h)_{pq}=\epsilon_{hpq4}-\big(\delta_{p4}\delta_{qh}-\delta_{ph}\delta_{q4}\big),\qquad '
         r'(t_h)_{pq}=\epsilon_{hpq4}+\big(\delta_{p4}\delta_{qh}-\delta_{ph}\delta_{q4}\big),\qquad h=1,2,3,\ p,q=1,\dots,4,')
    d.eq(r's_1=%s,\ s_2=%s,\ s_3=%s,' % (pmat(D['s4'][1]), pmat(D['s4'][2]), pmat(D['s4'][3])))
    d.eq(r't_1=%s,\ t_2=%s,\ t_3=%s,' % (pmat(D['t4'][1]), pmat(D['t4'][2]), pmat(D['t4'][3])))
    d.p('and from them the 8×8 matrices')
    d.align([r'\sigma&=\begin{pmatrix}0&1_4\\1_4&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad \zeta_0=1_8,\qquad '
             r'\zeta_h=\begin{pmatrix}0&s_h\\s_h&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad \zeta_{7-h}=\begin{pmatrix}0&t_h\\-t_h&0\end{pmatrix}\ (h=1,2,3),',
             r'\zeta_7&=\zeta_1\zeta_2\zeta_3\zeta_4\zeta_5\zeta_6=\mathrm{diag}(-1_4,1_4),\qquad \tau_A=\zeta_A,\qquad '
             r'\bar\tau_0=1_8,\quad \bar\tau_A=\sigma\,\tau_A^{\mathsf T}\sigma\ (A=1,\dots,7).'])
    taus = D['tau']
    d.eq(r'\tau_1=%s,\quad \tau_2=%s,' % (pmat(taus[1]), pmat(taus[2])))
    d.eq(r'\tau_3=%s,\quad \tau_4=%s,' % (pmat(taus[3]), pmat(taus[4])))
    d.eq(r'\tau_5=%s,\quad \tau_6=%s,' % (pmat(taus[5]), pmat(taus[6])))
    d.eq(r'\tau_7=%s.' % pmat(taus[7]))
    d.p('They satisfy (all verified in 7+1.nb exactly as in 4+4.nb)')
    d.align([r'\tfrac12\big(\zeta_A\zeta_B+\zeta_B\zeta_A\big)&=-\eta^{(4,4)}_{AB}\,1_8\quad(A,B=1,\dots,7),\qquad '
             r'-\eta^{(4,4)}_{AA}\,\zeta_A=\zeta_A^{\mathsf T},',
             r'\tfrac12\big(\tau_A\bar\tau_B+\tau_B\bar\tau_A\big)&=\tfrac12\big(\bar\tau_A\tau_B+\bar\tau_B\tau_A\big)=\eta^{(4,4)}_{AB}\,1_8\quad(A,B=0,\dots,7),',
             r'\tau_1\tau_2\cdots\tau_7&=1_8,\qquad \sigma=\tau_1\tau_2\tau_3=\tau_4\tau_5\tau_6\tau_7,\qquad \sigma^2=1_8,',
             r'\sigma\tau_h&=\tau_h\sigma\ (h=1,2,3),\qquad \sigma\tau_k=-\tau_k\sigma\ (k=4,\dots,7).'])
    d.p('So $\\tau_1,\\tau_2,\\tau_3$ are antisymmetric and square to $-1_8$; $\\tau_4,\\dots,\\tau_7$ are symmetric and square '
        'to $+1_8$: they are the left multiplications of the three imaginary and the four split units of the split octonions.')
    d.h2('5.2 The 16×16 Dirac matrices $T_A$ of 4+4.nb')
    d.align([r'T_A&=\begin{pmatrix}0&\bar\tau_A\\ \tau_A&0\end{pmatrix}\ (A=0,\dots,7),\qquad T_AT_B+T_BT_A=2\,\eta^{(4,4)}_{AB}\,1_{16},',
             r'T_8&=T_0T_1\cdots T_7=\mathrm{diag}(-1_8,1_8),\qquad \sigma_{16}=T_0T_1T_2T_3=\mathrm{diag}(-\sigma,\sigma),',
             r'P_L&=\tfrac12(1_{16}-T_8)=\mathrm{diag}(1_8,0),\qquad P_R=\tfrac12(1_{16}+T_8)=\mathrm{diag}(0,1_8).'])
    d.h2('5.3 How the split-octonion multiplication constants come from $\\tau_A$')
    d.p('Choose the unit spinor $u=(1,0,0,0,1,0,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}/\\sqrt2$, an eigenvector of $\\sigma$: $\\sigma u=u$, '
        '$u^{\\mathsf T}u=u^{\\mathsf T}\\sigma u=1$. The vielbein $E_{44}$ (4+4.nb: `E^A_a[hUSE]`) and its inverse are')
    d.eq(r'(E_{44})_{Ba}=\big(u^{\mathsf T}\sigma\,\bar\tau_B\big)_a,\qquad '
         r'E_{44}^{-1}=(u^{\mathsf T}\sigma u)\,\big(\eta^{(4,4)}_{00}\tau_0u,\ \eta^{(4,4)}_{11}\tau_1u,\ \dots,\ \eta^{(4,4)}_{77}\tau_7u\big)'
         r'\quad\text{(columns)}.')
    d.p('The left multiplication by the split unit $\\varepsilon_{B}$ (4+4.nb: `eA[B]`, $B=1,\\dots,8$) is the matrix '
        '$g_{BB}\\,E_{44}\\,\\tau_{B-1}\\,E_{44}^{-1}$ with $g=\\eta^{(4,4)}$:')
    d.eq(r'\varepsilon_B\,\varepsilon_C=\sum_{D=1}^{8}\Big(g_{BB}\,E_{44}\,\tau_{B-1}\,E_{44}^{-1}\Big)_{DC}\,\varepsilon_D .')
    d.p('In words: the split-octonion multiplication constants are the matrix elements of the 8×8 matrices $\\tau_A$, written '
        'in the octonion basis by the vielbein, times the metric sign $g_{BB}$. This is the table `gridx` of 4+4.nb '
        '(row $\\varepsilon_B$ times column $\\varepsilon_C$):')
    hdr, rows = D['split_table']
    d.table(hdr, rows, widths=[0.1] + [0.105] * 8, small=True)
    d.p('Split octonions: $\\varepsilon_1=1$, $\\varepsilon_2^2=\\varepsilon_3^2=\\varepsilon_4^2=-1$, '
        '$\\varepsilon_5^2=\\dots=\\varepsilon_8^2=+1$, norm $N_S(x)=x^{\\mathsf T}\\eta^{(4,4)}x$ with $N_S(xy)=N_S(x)N_S(y)$, '
        'alternative, and with zero divisors: $(\\varepsilon_1+\\varepsilon_5)(\\varepsilon_1-\\varepsilon_5)=0$.')

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    d.h1('6. The octonions: a real realization mirroring the split octonions')
    d.h2('6.1 From $\\tau$ to $\\tau^{O}$')
    d.p('All seven imaginary octonion units must square to $-1$, but $\\tau_4,\\dots,\\tau_7$ square to $+1$. Because $\\sigma$ '
        'commutes with $\\tau_1,\\tau_2,\\tau_3$, anticommutes with $\\tau_4,\\dots,\\tau_7$ and squares to $1_8$,')
    d.eq(r'(\tau_k\sigma)(\tau_k\sigma)=-\tau_k\tau_k\,\sigma\sigma=-1_8\qquad(k=4,\dots,7),')
    d.p('and all anticommutation relations survive. Over $\\mathbb C$ one would multiply $\\tau_k$ by $i$; the real matrix '
        '$\\sigma$ replaces that $i$. Definition:')
    d.eq(r'\tau^{O}_0=1_8,\qquad \tau^{O}_A=\tau_A\ (A=1,2,3),\qquad \tau^{O}_A=\tau_A\,\sigma\ (A=4,\dots,7),\qquad '
         r'\bar\tau^{O}_A=g^{O}(\tau^{O}_A)^{\mathsf T}g^{O}=(\tau^{O}_A)^{\mathsf T}.')
    tO = D['tauO']
    d.eq(r'\tau^{O}_4=%s,\quad \tau^{O}_5=%s,' % (pmat(tO[4]), pmat(tO[5])))
    d.eq(r'\tau^{O}_6=%s,\quad \tau^{O}_7=%s,' % (pmat(tO[6]), pmat(tO[7])))
    d.p('and $\\tau^{O}_1=\\tau_1$, $\\tau^{O}_2=\\tau_2$, $\\tau^{O}_3=\\tau_3$ as displayed in section 5.1. Properties '
        '(verified):')
    d.align([r'(\tau^{O}_i)^{\mathsf T}&=-\tau^{O}_i,\qquad (\tau^{O}_i)^2=-1_8,\qquad \tau^{O}_i\tau^{O}_j=-\tau^{O}_j\tau^{O}_i\ (i\neq j),',
             r'\tau^{O}_A\bar\tau^{O}_B+\tau^{O}_B\bar\tau^{O}_A&=\bar\tau^{O}_A\tau^{O}_B+\bar\tau^{O}_B\tau^{O}_A=2\,g^{O}_{AB}\,1_8=2\,\delta_{AB}\,1_8,',
             r'\tau^{O}_1\tau^{O}_2\cdots\tau^{O}_7&=1_8\qquad(\text{as }\tau_1\cdots\tau_7=1_8\text{ in 4+4.nb}),',
             r'\tau^{O}_4&=\tau_5\tau_6\tau_7,\quad \tau^{O}_5=-\tau_4\tau_6\tau_7,\quad \tau^{O}_6=\tau_4\tau_5\tau_7,\quad \tau^{O}_7=-\tau_4\tau_5\tau_6.'])
    d.p('For $x=\\sum_Ax_Ae_A$ put $\\tau^{O}(x)=\\sum_Ax_A\\tau^{O}_A$; then $\\tau^{O}(x)\\,\\bar\\tau^{O}(x)=N(x)\\,1_8$ with '
        '$N(x)=x_0^2+\\dots+x_7^2$, the matrix form of $x\\bar x=N(x)$. In 4+4.nb the same relation holds with $\\eta^{(4,4)}$, '
        'the split norm.')
    d.h2('6.2 The octonion vielbein (same unit spinor $u$)')
    d.p('In 4+4.nb the rows of the vielbein are $u^{\\mathsf T}\\sigma\\bar\\tau_B$, with the split spinor metric $\\sigma$. For '
        'the octonions the spinor metric is Euclidean ($\\sigma\\to g^{O}=1_8$), so')
    d.eq(r'(E^{O})_{Ba}=\big(u^{\mathsf T}g^{O}\bar\tau^{O}_B\big)_a,\qquad (E^{O})^{-1}=(E^{O})^{\mathsf T}=(u^{\mathsf T}g^{O}u)\big(g^{O}_{00}\tau^{O}_0u,\dots,g^{O}_{77}\tau^{O}_7u\big),\qquad E^{O}=\eta^{(4,4)}E_{44}.')
    d.p('Because $\\sigma u=u$, $\\tau^{O}_Au=\\tau_Au$ for every $A$: the two vielbeins differ only by the signs of rows 5–8.')
    d.eq(r'E^{O}=%s .' % pmat(D['EO']))
    d.h2('6.3 The octonion multiplication constants')
    d.p('Exactly the formula of 4+4.nb, with $\\tau\\to\\tau^{O}$, $E_{44}\\to E^{O}$, $g\\to g^{O}$ and a 0-based basis '
        '$e_A\\leftrightarrow\\tau^{O}_A\\leftrightarrow x_A$:')
    d.eq(r'L^{O}_A=g^{O}_{AA}\,E^{O}\,\tau^{O}_A\,(E^{O})^{-1},\qquad e_A\,e_K=\sum_{L=0}^{7}\big(L^{O}_A\big)_{LK}\,e_L,'
         r'\qquad (xy)_L=\sum_{A,K}x_A\,y_K\,\big(L^{O}_A\big)_{LK}.')
    d.p('Every $L^{O}_A$ is a signed permutation matrix, so every multiplication constant is $0$ or $\\pm1$. The table '
        '(row $e_A$ times column $e_K$; Mathematica `gridO`, the mirror of `gridx`):')
    hdr, rows = D['oct_table']
    d.table(hdr, rows, widths=[0.1] + [0.105] * 8, small=True)
    d.h2('6.4 The properties of [1A], verified')
    d.p('**Units.** $e_0$ is a two-sided identity, $e_i^2=-e_0$ and $e_ie_j=-e_je_i$ for $i\\neq j$ ($i,j=1,\\dots,7$).')
    d.p('**Fano plane.** Each product of two distinct imaginary units is $\\pm$ a third one. The seven triples are the lines '
        'of the Fano plane; written in the cyclic order $(a,b,c)$ with $e_ae_b=+e_c$ (and then $e_be_c=e_a$, $e_ce_a=e_b$):')
    d.eq(r'(1,3,2),\ (1,5,4),\ (1,6,7),\ (2,6,4),\ (2,7,5),\ (3,5,6),\ (3,7,4).')
    d.p('Every pair of distinct imaginary units lies on exactly one line, and each line together with $e_0$ spans a copy of the '
        'quaternions (closed and associative). In a picture: put $e_1$ at the centre, $e_2,e_4,e_7$ at the vertices of a '
        'triangle and $e_6,e_3,e_5$ at the midpoints of the sides $e_2e_4$, $e_4e_7$, $e_7e_2$; the lines are the three sides, '
        'the three medians and the inscribed circle through $e_3,e_5,e_6$.')
    d.p('**Algebraic laws** (proved symbolically for general octonions $x,y,z$ with 8 symbolic components each; the Moufang '
        'identities, being linear in $x$ and $y$, are proved for basis units $x,y$ and symbolic $z$):')
    d.align([r'x(xy)&=(xx)y,\qquad (yx)x=y(xx),\qquad (xy)x=x(yx),\qquad x(xx)=(xx)x,',
             r'[x,y,z]&:=(xy)z-x(yz)=-[y,x,z]=-[x,z,y],',
             r'z\big(x(zy)\big)&=\big((zx)z\big)y,\qquad x\big(z(yz)\big)=\big((xz)y\big)z,\qquad (zx)(yz)=\big(z(xy)\big)z,',
             r'x\bar x&=\bar x x=N(x)\,e_0,\qquad \overline{xy}=\bar y\,\bar x,\qquad \mathrm{Re}(x)=\tfrac12(x+\bar x)=x_0,',
             r'N(xy)&=N(x)\,N(y),\qquad x\,x^{-1}=x^{-1}x=e_0\ \ \text{for}\ \ x^{-1}=\bar x/N(x),',
             r'x^2&-2\,\mathrm{Re}(x)\,x+N(x)\,e_0=0 .'])
    d.p('The last identity shows that the subalgebra generated by one octonion is $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$ '
        '(power-associativity). Multiplicativity of the norm is Degen\'s eight-square identity: with $x=\\sum a_Ae_A$ and '
        '$y=\\sum b_Ae_A$ the components of $xy$ are')
    xy = D['xy']
    d.align([r'(xy)_{%d}&=%s' % (k, sp.latex(xy[k])) + (',' if k < 7 else ',') for k in range(8)])
    d.eq(r'\Big(\sum_{A=0}^{7}a_A^2\Big)\Big(\sum_{A=0}^{7}b_A^2\Big)=\sum_{L=0}^{7}(xy)_L^2 .')
    d.p('Hence $N(x)=0$ only for $x=0$, and $xy=0$ only if $x=0$ or $y=0$: no zero divisors, a division algebra '
        '(unlike the split octonions).')
    d.p('**Non-associativity.** Exactly 168 of the 512 ordered basis triples $(e_A,e_B,e_K)$ are non-associative: the triples of '
        'distinct imaginary units not on a common Fano line ($7\\cdot6\\cdot4=168$). Example: $(e_1e_2)e_4=e_7$ but '
        '$e_1(e_2e_4)=-e_7$.')
    n = D['num']
    d.p('**A numerical example.** For $x=%s$, $y=%s$, $z=%s$:' % (n['x'], n['y'], n['z']))
    d.align([r'xy&=%s,' % n['xy'],
             r'yx&=%s,' % n['yx'],
             r'N(x)&=%d,\quad N(y)=%d,\quad N(xy)=%d=N(x)N(y),' % (n['Nx'], n['Ny'], n['Nxy']),
             r'x^{-1}&=%s,' % n['xinv'],
             r'(xy)z-x(yz)&=%s\neq0 .' % n['assoc']])
    d.h2('6.5 Cayley–Dickson doubling $\\mathbb O=\\mathbb H\\oplus\\ell\\mathbb H$')
    d.p('The span of $e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3$ — the time direction and the three ordinary space directions $x_0,\\dots,x_3$ — is a '
        'quaternion subalgebra $\\mathbb H$, and $\\ell=e_4$ doubles it: $e_4e_k=e_{4+k}$ ($k=1,2,3$), $e_4^2=-e_0$. Every '
        'octonion is $x=a+\\ell b$ with quaternions $a=(x_0,x_1,x_2,x_3)$ and $b=(x_4,x_5,x_6,x_7)$. The orientation inherited '
        'from 4+4.nb is $e_1e_2=-e_3$ (so $i=e_1$, $j=e_2$, $k=-e_3$ are Hamilton\'s units). With $\\ell^2=\\gamma$ the product is')
    d.align([r'(a,b)(c,d)&=\big(ac+\gamma\,d\bar b,\ \ \bar a d+cb\big),',
             r'\gamma&=-1:\ \text{octonions (this document)},\qquad \gamma=+1:\ \text{split octonions (4+4.nb)}.'])
    d.p('Both cases are proved symbolically in 7+1.nb. The formula $(ac-\\bar d b,\\ \\bar a d+cb)$ of [1A] fails: for '
        '$x=(1,1)$, $y=(i,0)$ it gives $x^2y=(0,2i)$ but $x(xy)=(2i,2i)$, and its norm is not multiplicative.')
    d.h2('6.6 Octonions versus split octonions')
    d.p('With the split units relabelled 0-based ($\\varepsilon_{B+1}\\to e^{S}_B$), the two tables differ only in the products '
        'of two doubled units:')
    d.eq(r'e_Ae_B\big|_{\mathbb O}=s_{AB}\;e^{S}_Ae^{S}_B\big|_{\text{split}},\qquad s_{AB}=\begin{cases}-1,&A\ge4\ \text{and}\ B\ge4,\\ +1,&\text{otherwise},\end{cases}')
    d.p('because $\\ell^2=-1$ instead of $+1$. Over $\\mathbb C$ the algebras coincide: $\\varphi(e^{S}_A)=c_Ae_A$ with '
        '$c=(1,1,1,1,i,i,i,i)$ satisfies $\\varphi(xy)=\\varphi(x)\\varphi(y)$ and $N_{\\mathbb O}(\\varphi(x))=N_S(x)$, so '
        '$\\mathbb O_{\\text{split}}\\otimes\\mathbb C\\cong\\mathbb O\\otimes\\mathbb C$. The real substitute for the factors $i$ '
        'is the matrix $\\sigma$ of section 6.1.')

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    d.h1('7. Clifford algebras from the octonions')
    d.h2('7.1 The exact mirror of $T_A$: the Euclidean algebra $Cl(8,0)$')
    d.p('Copying $T_A=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&\\bar\\tau_A\\\\\\tau_A&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$ with $\\tau\\to\\tau^{O}$:')
    d.align([r'T^{O}_A&=\begin{pmatrix}0&\bar\tau^{O}_A\\ \tau^{O}_A&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad T^{O}_AT^{O}_B+T^{O}_BT^{O}_A=2\,g^{O}_{AB}\,1_{16}=2\,\delta_{AB}\,1_{16}\qquad(Cl(8,0)\cong\mathbb R(16)),',
             r'T^{O}_A&=T_A\ (A=0,1,2,3),\qquad T^{O}_k=T_k\,S_{44}\ (k=4,\dots,7),\qquad S_{44}=T_4T_5T_6T_7=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma,\sigma),',
             r'T^{O}_8&=T^{O}_0T^{O}_1\cdots T^{O}_7=T_8=\mathrm{diag}(-1_8,1_8).'])
    d.h2('7.2 The real Clifford algebra of 7+1 spacetime: $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}$, $Cl(1,7)\\cong\\mathbb R(16)$')
    d.p('**Definition.**')
    d.align([r'\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0&=T^{O}_0=\begin{pmatrix}0&1_8\\1_8&0\end{pmatrix}\ \ \text{(time)},\qquad '
             r'\Gamma^{(1,7)}_i=T^{O}_0\,T^{O}_i=\begin{pmatrix}\tau^{O}_i&0\\0&\bar\tau^{O}_i\end{pmatrix}\ \ (i=1,\dots,7,\ \text{space}),',
             r'\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0&=T_0,\qquad \Gamma^{(1,7)}_i=T_0\,T_i\ (i=1,2,3),\qquad \Gamma^{(1,7)}_k=T_0\,T_k\,S_{44}\ (k=4,\dots,7)\qquad\text{(only 4+4.nb matrices)}.'])
    d.p('**Relations** (all verified):')
    d.align([r'\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A\Gamma^{(1,7)}_B+\Gamma^{(1,7)}_B\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A&=2\,\eta^{(1,7)}_{AB}\,1_{16}=-2\,\eta^{(7,1)}_{AB}\,1_{16},',
             r'(\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0)^{\mathsf T}&=\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0,\quad (\Gamma^{(1,7)}_i)^{\mathsf T}=-\Gamma^{(1,7)}_i,',
             r'(\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A)^{\mathsf T}&=\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0\quad\text{(real Dirac adjoint)},',
             r'\mathrm{tr}\big(\Gamma^{(1,7)}_{A_1}\cdots\Gamma^{(1,7)}_{A_k}\big)&=0\quad(1\le k\le8,\ A_1<\dots<A_k).'])
    d.p('All entries are $0$ or $\\pm1$. **Why this is the Clifford algebra of [1B].** (i) For $v=\\sum_Av_Ae_A$ the matrix '
        '$\\Gamma^{(1,7)}(v)=\\sum_Av_A\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$ satisfies')
    d.eq(r'\Gamma^{(1,7)}(v)^2=Q(v)\,1_{16},\qquad Q(v)=v^{\mathsf T}\eta^{(1,7)}v=v_0^2-v_1^2-\dots-v_7^2,')
    d.p('so by the universal property of $Cl(V,Q)=\\mathcal T(V)/\\mathcal I_Q(V)$ the map extends to an algebra homomorphism '
        '$Cl(V,Q)\\to\\mathbb R(16)$. (ii) The 256 ordered products $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_{A_1}\\cdots\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_{A_k}$ '
        '($A_1<\\dots<A_k$, $k=0,\\dots,8$, counted by $\\binom8k$) are linearly independent, so the homomorphism is injective; both '
        'sides have dimension 256, hence $Cl(1,7)\\cong\\mathbb R(16)$. (iii) Only multiples of $1_{16}$ commute with all '
        '$\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$: the representation is irreducible over $\\mathbb R$.')
    d.h2('7.3 The complexified realization in the exact block form of 4+4.nb: $\\Gamma^{(7,1)}$')
    d.p('For the sign convention of 4+4.nb, $\\Gamma_A\\Gamma_B+\\Gamma_B\\Gamma_A=2\\eta^{(7,1)}_{AB}1_{16}$, complex numbers are '
        'unavoidable (section 7.4). The complexification needed is minimal: multiply the time block by $i$.')
    d.align([r'\tau^{C}_0&=\bar\tau^{C}_0=i\,1_8,\qquad \tau^{C}_k=\tau^{O}_k,\quad \bar\tau^{C}_k=\bar\tau^{O}_k\ (k=1,\dots,7),\qquad '
             r'\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A=\begin{pmatrix}0&\bar\tau^{C}_A\\ \tau^{C}_A&0\end{pmatrix},',
             r'\Gamma^{(7,1)}_0&=i\,T_0,\qquad \Gamma^{(7,1)}_k=T^{O}_k\ (k=1,\dots,7),\qquad '
             r'\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A\Gamma^{(7,1)}_B+\Gamma^{(7,1)}_B\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A=2\,\eta^{(7,1)}_{AB}\,1_{16},',
             r'\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A&=S\,\big(i\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A\big)\,S^{-1},\qquad S=\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\big(1_{16}-i\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0\big),\qquad SS^{\dagger}=1_{16}.'])
    d.p('This is literally the formula $T_A=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&\\bar\\tau_A\\\\\\tau_A&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$ of 4+4.nb, '
        'applied to the complexified 8×8 matrices; only the time component is complex.')
    d.h2('7.4 Theorem: with $\\eta^{(7,1)}$ there is no real 16×16 realization')
    d.p('**Theorem.** There are no real 16×16 matrices $R_0,\\dots,R_7$ with $R_AR_B+R_BR_A=2\\eta^{(7,1)}_{AB}1_{16}$. Reordering, '
        'renaming or changing the signs of the indices cannot help, because none of these changes the signature $(7,1)$.')
    d.p('**Proof.** $Cl_8(\\mathbb C)\\cong\\mathbb C(16)$ has exactly one irreducible module, $\\mathbb C^{16}$. If real $R_A$ '
        'existed, $R_A$ and $\\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A$ would be two 16-dimensional modules, so $R_A=T\\,\\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A\\,T^{-1}$ for some '
        'invertible complex $T$. Since $R_A$ is real, complex conjugation gives')
    d.eq(r'M\,\overline{\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A}=\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A\,M\quad(A=0,\dots,7),\qquad M=T^{-1}\,\overline{T},\qquad M\,\overline M=1_{16}.')
    d.p('By Schur\'s lemma all such $M$ form a one-dimensional space. Solving the linear equations (7.4 of the notebook) gives '
        'exactly that space, spanned by the volume element')
    d.eq(r'M_0=\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8=\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0\Gamma^{(1,7)}_1\cdots\Gamma^{(1,7)}_7=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1_8\\1_8&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad M_0\,\overline{M_0}=M_0^2=-1_{16}.')
    d.p('Hence $M=cM_0$ and $M\\overline M=-|c|^2\\,1_{16}\\neq1_{16}$, a contradiction. $\\square$ The sign of $M\\overline M$ is a '
        'Frobenius–Schur-type indicator: $+1$ means the complex module has a real form (the algebra is $\\mathbb R(16)$), $-1$ '
        'means it is quaternionic (the algebra is $\\mathbb H(8)$, whose smallest real module is $\\mathbb H^8=\\mathbb R^{32}$). For '
        '$\\Gamma^{(1,7)}$ the indicator is $+1$ (take $M=1_{16}$).')
    d.h2('7.5 A real realization of $Cl(7,1)$ needs 32×32 matrices')
    d.align([r'\widehat\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A&=\varepsilon\otimes\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A,\qquad \varepsilon=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad '
             r'\widehat\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A\widehat\Gamma^{(7,1)}_B+\widehat\Gamma^{(7,1)}_B\widehat\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A=2\,\eta^{(7,1)}_{AB}\,1_{32},',
             r'Q_i&=\varepsilon\otimes1_{16},\qquad Q_j=\sigma_1\otimes\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8,\qquad Q_k=\sigma_3\otimes\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8,',
             r'Q_i^2&=Q_j^2=Q_k^2=-1_{32},\qquad Q_iQ_j=Q_k=-Q_jQ_i,\qquad Q\,\widehat\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A=\widehat\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A\,Q\ \ (Q=Q_i,Q_j,Q_k).'])
    d.p('The matrices commuting with all $\\widehat\\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A$ form exactly the 4-dimensional span of $1_{32},Q_i,Q_j,Q_k$ '
        '(computed in the notebook): the quaternions. This is $Cl(7,1)\\cong\\mathbb H(8)$ acting on $\\mathbb R^{32}=\\mathbb H^8$.')
    d.h2('7.6 Volume elements and chirality')
    d.align([r'T_8^2&=+1_{16}\ \Rightarrow\ P_{L,R}=\tfrac12(1_{16}\mp T_8)\ \text{real (4+4.nb)};\qquad (T^{O}_8)^2=+1_{16},\ T^{O}_8=T_8,',
             r'\big(\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8\big)^2&=-1_{16},\qquad \Gamma^{(1,7)}_8\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A=-\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8,\qquad '
             r'\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8\Sigma_{AB}=\Sigma_{AB}\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8,',
             r'\chi^{(7,1)}&=-i\,\Gamma^{(7,1)}_8=T^{O}_8=T_8,\qquad \tfrac12\big(1_{16}\mp\chi^{(7,1)}\big)=P_{L,R},',
             r'\chi^{(1,7)}&=i\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8,\qquad \tfrac12\big(1_{16}\mp\chi^{(1,7)}\big)\ \text{complex projectors of rank 8 each}.'])
    d.p('With real matrices in 7+1 dimensions there is no real chirality projector: $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8$ is a complex structure '
        'commuting with Spin(1,7), so the real 16-component spinor is one complex 8-component Weyl spinor. In the basis '
        'of $\\Gamma^{(7,1)}$ the chiral projectors are literally $P_L$ and $P_R$ of 4+4.nb.')
    d.h2('7.7 What the octonions do: $\\Gamma(x)^2=\\mathrm{Re}(xx)$ and alternativity')
    d.align([r'\mathrm{Re}(x\,x)&=x_0^2-x_1^2-\dots-x_7^2=x^{\mathsf T}\eta^{(1,7)}x,\qquad \Gamma^{(1,7)}(x)^2=\mathrm{Re}(x\,x)\,1_{16},',
             r'T^{O}(x)^2&=N(x)\,1_{16}=x^{\mathsf T}g^{O}x\,1_{16},\qquad T(x)^2=x^{\mathsf T}\eta^{(4,4)}x\,1_{16}\ \text{(4+4.nb)}.'])
    d.p('Rotating both 8-blocks by $E^{O}$ gives $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}$ in the octonion basis,')
    d.eq(r'\Gamma^{e}_0=\begin{pmatrix}0&1_8\\1_8&0\end{pmatrix},\quad \Gamma^{e}_i=\begin{pmatrix}L^{O}_i&0\\0&-L^{O}_i\end{pmatrix},\qquad '
         r'\Gamma^{e}(x)\begin{pmatrix}\psi_1\\ \psi_2\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}x_0\psi_2+\vec x\,\psi_1\\ x_0\psi_1-\vec x\,\psi_2\end{pmatrix}\quad(\psi_1,\psi_2\in\mathbb O),')
    d.eq(r'\Gamma^{e}(x)^2\begin{pmatrix}\psi_1\\ \psi_2\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}x_0^2\psi_1+\vec x(\vec x\psi_1)\\ x_0^2\psi_2+\vec x(\vec x\psi_2)\end{pmatrix}=(x_0^2-|\vec x|^2)\begin{pmatrix}\psi_1\\ \psi_2\end{pmatrix},')
    d.p('where $\\vec x=\\sum_{i}x_ie_i$ and the last step is alternativity, $\\vec x(\\vec x\\psi)=(\\vec x\\vec x)\\psi=-|\\vec x|^2\\psi$. '
        'The Clifford relation of 7+1 spacetime is the alternativity of the octonions.')

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    d.h1('8. Examples that test everything')
    d.h2('8.1 The Lorentz algebra so(1,7)')
    d.align([r'\Sigma_{AB}&=\tfrac14\big(\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A\Gamma^{(1,7)}_B-\Gamma^{(1,7)}_B\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A\big)\quad(A<B:\ 28\ \text{real, linearly independent matrices}),',
             r'\big[\Sigma_{AB},\Gamma^{(1,7)}_K\big]&=\eta^{(1,7)}_{BK}\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A-\eta^{(1,7)}_{AK}\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_B,',
             r'\big[\Sigma_{AB},\Sigma_{KL}\big]&=\eta^{(1,7)}_{BK}\Sigma_{AL}-\eta^{(1,7)}_{AK}\Sigma_{BL}-\eta^{(1,7)}_{BL}\Sigma_{AK}+\eta^{(1,7)}_{AL}\Sigma_{BK}.'])
    d.h2('8.2 A boost along $x_1$ and a rotation in the $x_4$–$x_5$ plane')
    d.p('Because $(\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_1)^2=+1_{16}$ and $(\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_4\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_5)^2=-1_{16}$,')
    d.align([r'S(\beta)&=e^{\beta\Sigma_{01}}=\cosh\tfrac\beta2\,1_{16}+\sinh\tfrac\beta2\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0\Gamma^{(1,7)}_1,\qquad '
             r'S(\beta)^{-1}=\cosh\tfrac\beta2\,1_{16}-\sinh\tfrac\beta2\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0\Gamma^{(1,7)}_1,',
             r'R(\theta)&=e^{\theta\Sigma_{45}}=\cos\tfrac\theta2\,1_{16}+\sin\tfrac\theta2\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_4\Gamma^{(1,7)}_5,\qquad '
             r'R(\theta)^{-1}=\cos\tfrac\theta2\,1_{16}-\sin\tfrac\theta2\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_4\Gamma^{(1,7)}_5,'])
    d.p('both real (they solve $dS/d\\beta=\\Sigma_{01}S$, $S(0)=1_{16}$ and $dR/d\\theta=\\Sigma_{45}R$, $R(0)=1_{16}$). They act on the '
        'generators as Lorentz transformations, with $\\Lambda$ read off from traces:')
    d.eq(r'S\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_K\,S^{-1}=\sum_{B=0}^{7}\Lambda_{BK}\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_B,\qquad '
         r'\Lambda_{BK}=\tfrac1{16}\,\mathrm{tr}\big(\eta^{(1,7)}_{BB}\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_B\,S\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_K\,S^{-1}\big),\qquad '
         r'\Lambda^{\mathsf T}\eta^{(1,7)}\Lambda=\eta^{(1,7)},')
    d.eq(r'\Lambda_{\text{boost}}=\begin{pmatrix}\cosh\beta&-\sinh\beta&0\\-\sinh\beta&\cosh\beta&0\\0&0&1_6\end{pmatrix},\qquad '
         r'\Lambda_{\text{rot}}=\begin{pmatrix}1_4&0&0\\0&\begin{smallmatrix}\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\ \sin\theta&\cos\theta\end{smallmatrix}&0\\0&0&1_2\end{pmatrix}.')
    d.h2('8.3 The 7+1 Dirac operator squares to the wave operator')
    d.eq(r'\Gamma^{A}=\eta_{(1,7)}^{AA}\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A,\qquad \big(\Gamma^{A}\partial_A\big)^2\Psi=\eta_{(1,7)}^{AB}\,\partial_A\partial_B\Psi='
         r'\big(\partial_0^2-\partial_1^2-\dots-\partial_7^2\big)\Psi')
    d.p('for 16 completely arbitrary functions $\\Psi=(\\Psi_1,\\dots,\\Psi_{16})$ of $x_0,\\dots,x_7$ (the notebook proves it '
        'symbolically; here $\\eta_{(1,7)}^{AB}$ is the inverse metric, equal to $\\eta^{(1,7)}_{AB}$).')
    d.h2('8.4 Spinors on the mass shell: real amplitudes')
    d.p('Take $p=(3,2,2,0,0,0,0,0)$ (contravariant components $p^A$) and $m=1$: $p^{\\mathsf T}\\eta^{(1,7)}p=9-4-4=1=m^2$. With '
        '$\\not p=\\sum_Ap^A\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$ we have $\\not p^{\\,2}=m^2\\,1_{16}$, so $\\not p$ has eigenvalues $\\pm m$, eight times '
        'each. Because $\\not p$ is a real matrix, $(\\not p-m)w=0$ has an 8-dimensional space of REAL solutions $w$ (spinor '
        'amplitudes). The basis printed by Mathematica (`NullSpace`, one solution per row):')
    W = D['nb']['W']
    if W is not None:
        d.eq(r'W=%s,\qquad (\not p-m)\,W^{\mathsf T}=0 .' % pmat(W))
    d.p('Each row $w$ gives the plane wave $\\Psi=w\\,e^{-i\\,p\\cdot x}$, $p\\cdot x=p^{\\mathsf T}\\eta^{(1,7)}x$, solving '
        '$(i\\Gamma^{A}\\partial_A-m)\\Psi=0$. The plane wave itself is complex: real amplitudes do not make a real field '
        '(section 8.5).')
    d.h2('8.5 Real spinor fields: massless with 16 components, massive with 32')
    d.p('The operator $i\\Gamma^{A}\\partial_A$ is imaginary, so a real $\\Psi$ cannot solve $(i\\Gamma^{A}\\partial_A-m)\\Psi=0$. '
        'A real first-order equation $(G^{A}\\partial_A-m)\\Psi=0$ with real matrices $G_A$ ($G^{A}=\\eta_{(7,1)}^{AA}G_A$) implies '
        'the Klein–Gordon equation, through')
    d.eq(r'\big(G^{A}\partial_A+m\big)\big(G^{A}\partial_A-m\big)=-\big(\partial_0^2-\partial_1^2-\dots-\partial_7^2+m^2\big),')
    d.p('only if $G_AG_B+G_BG_A=2\\,\\eta^{(7,1)}_{AB}\\,1$ (time squaring to $-1$), and that relation has no real 16×16 solution '
        '(section 7.4). The remaining real option, $(\\Gamma^{A}\\partial_A-mJ)\\Psi=0$, squares to the Klein–Gordon operator '
        '$\\partial_0^2-\\partial_1^2-\\dots-\\partial_7^2+m^2$ only if the real matrix $J$ anticommutes with every '
        '$\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$ and $J^2=+1_{16}$. But (computed in the notebook)')
    d.eq(r'\big\lbrace J\in\mathbb R(16):\ J\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A=-\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A\,J\ \ (A=0,\dots,7)\big\rbrace=\mathbb R\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8,'
         r'\qquad \big(c\,\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8\big)^2=-c^2\,1_{16},')
    d.p('and with $J=c\\,\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8$ the square is $\\partial_0^2-\\partial_1^2-\\dots-\\partial_7^2-c^2m^2$, a tachyon. '
        'So with 16 real components only massless real fields exist. For the null momentum $k=(1,1,0,0,0,0,0,0)$, '
        '$k^{\\mathsf T}\\eta^{(1,7)}k=0$ and $\\not k=\\sum_Ak^A\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$ satisfies $\\not k^{\\,2}=0$; the real equation '
        '$\\not k\\,w=0$ has 8 real solutions, and for each of them')
    d.eq(r'\Psi=w\cos(k\cdot x),\qquad \Gamma^{A}\partial_A\Psi=-\sin(k\cdot x)\,\not k\,w=0\qquad(\text{a real solution}).')
    d.p('A real massive field needs the real 32×32 matrices $\\widehat\\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A=\\varepsilon\\otimes\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$ of '
        'section 7.5, which do satisfy the $\\eta^{(7,1)}$ relation. With $\\widehat\\Gamma^{A}=\\eta_{(7,1)}^{AA}\\widehat\\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A$ '
        'and $P=\\sum_Ap^A\\widehat\\Gamma^{(7,1)}_A$ ($p$ as in section 8.4):')
    d.align([r'\big(\widehat\Gamma^{A}\partial_A\big)^2&=-\big(\partial_0^2-\partial_1^2-\dots-\partial_7^2\big)\,1_{32},\qquad '
             r'P^2=\big(p^{\mathsf T}\eta^{(7,1)}p\big)\,1_{32}=-m^2\,1_{32},',
             r'\Psi&=w\cos(p\cdot x)+\tfrac1m\,P\,w\,\sin(p\cdot x)\qquad(w\in\mathbb R^{32}\ \text{arbitrary}),',
             r'\big(\widehat\Gamma^{A}\partial_A-m\big)\Psi&=0,\qquad \big(\partial_0^2-\partial_1^2-\dots-\partial_7^2+m^2\big)\Psi=0.'])
    d.p('Derivation: with $\\phi=p\\cdot x$ one has $\\partial_A\\phi=(\\eta^{(1,7)}p)_A$ and $\\eta_{(7,1)}^{AA}\\eta^{(1,7)}_{AA}=-1$, '
        'so $\\widehat\\Gamma^{A}\\partial_A\\Psi=-P\\big(-w\\sin\\phi+\\tfrac1mPw\\cos\\phi\\big)=Pw\\sin\\phi+m\\,w\\cos\\phi=m\\Psi$. '
        'The 32 real components are the quaternionic module $\\mathbb H^8=\\mathbb R^{32}$ of $Cl(7,1)\\cong\\mathbb H(8)$.')
    d.h2('8.6 All nine signatures $p+q=8$')
    d.p('Complex generators of every signature: the first $p$ of the $T^{O}_A$ and $i$ times the remaining $q$. The indicator of '
        'section 7.4 then decides $\\mathbb R(16)$ or $\\mathbb H(8)$ (table of section 4.3). Real 16×16 generators exist exactly '
        'in the five $\\mathbb R(16)$ cases, and all five are built here from the 4+4 and octonion matrices:')
    d.align([r'Cl(8,0)&:\ T^{O}_A,\qquad Cl(4,4):\ T_A\ \text{(4+4.nb)},\qquad Cl(1,7):\ \Gamma^{(1,7)}_A,',
             r'Cl(5,3)&:\ \Gamma^{(5,3)}_0=T_0,\ \ \Gamma^{(5,3)}_i=T_0T_i\ (i=1,\dots,7),\qquad \eta^{(5,3)}=\mathrm{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1,1,1,1,1),',
             r'&\phantom{:}\ \Gamma^{(5,3)}_A\Gamma^{(5,3)}_B+\Gamma^{(5,3)}_B\Gamma^{(5,3)}_A=2\,\eta^{(5,3)}_{AB}1_{16},',
             r'Cl(0,8)&:\ \Gamma^{(0,8)}_A=T^{O}_8\,T^{O}_A,\qquad \Gamma^{(0,8)}_A\Gamma^{(0,8)}_B+\Gamma^{(0,8)}_B\Gamma^{(0,8)}_A=-2\,\delta_{AB}1_{16}.'])
    d.p('$\\Gamma^{(5,3)}$ is the split-octonion analogue of $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}$: its quadratic form $x^{\\mathsf T}\\eta^{(5,3)}x$ is '
        '$\\mathrm{Re}(xx)$ of the split octonions, just as $x^{\\mathsf T}\\eta^{(1,7)}x$ is $\\mathrm{Re}(xx)$ of the octonions.')

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    d.h1('9. Verification')
    allc = all_checks(D)
    d.p('**In Mathematica.** 7+1.nb was evaluated in %s by `tools/run_eval.wl` (all cells evaluated in a fresh kernel, then saved), '
        'in the private context `%s`. It contains %d input cells and %d checks `check["name", test]`: %d print their result where '
        'they stand, and %d are made by the function `frameChecks` of section 13.1 (nine checks for each of four metrics) and printed '
        'by the summary table of section 20. All %d returned True, the summary cells printed `failedChecks = {}` and '
        '`allChecksPassed = True`, there were %d message cells, and the evaluation took %s s.'
        % (nbinfo['version'], nbinfo['context'], nbinfo['inputs'], len(allc), len(nbinfo['checks']), len(allc) - len(nbinfo['checks']),
           sum(1 for c in allc if c[1] == 'True'), nbinfo['messages'], log.get('seconds', '?')))
    from collections import OrderedDict
    by = OrderedDict()
    for name, res, sec, sub in allc:
        by.setdefault(sec, [0, 0]); by[sec][0] += 1; by[sec][1] += (res == 'True')
    d.table(['notebook section', 'checks', 'True'], [[k, str(v[0]), str(v[1])] for k, v in by.items()], widths=[0.66, 0.12, 0.12])
    d.p('**Independently in Python** (exact arithmetic with sympy, in `tools/`): `ref44.py` rebuilds every 4+4.nb object and '
        'reproduces the split table of 4+4.nb; `ref71.py` and `extra71.py` rebuild every 7+1 object and re-prove the relations; '
        '`verify_7p1_nb.py` parses the evaluated 7+1.nb and confirms that the matrices $\\tau_A$, $\\tau^{O}_A$, '
        '$\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$, the two multiplication tables, the matrices $\\Lambda$, the eight real amplitudes $W$ and the '
        'classification table printed by Mathematica coincide with the Python values. For sections 7–9, `ref_dirac16.py` repeats every '
        'construction (sympy, mpmath with 30 digits, numpy) with %d checks, all True, and `verify_7p1_nb.py` compares the printed spinors, '
        'the printed closed-form integrals and 23 printed numbers with it (agreement to at least 17 digits) and confirms that sections 0–6 '
        'are cell for cell the notebook of the first stage.' % len(D['ref_bool']))
    if D.get('s3'):
        d.p(S3.verification_text(D['s3']))

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    d.h1('10. The notebook 7+1.nb')
    d.ul(['**0. How to use this notebook** — evaluation, the `check` bookkeeping, the private context, index conventions.',
          '**1. Conventions, coordinates and notation** — the coordinate table, the metrics $\\eta^{(4,4)}$, $\\eta^{(7,1)}$, $\\eta^{(1,7)}$, $g^{O}$, '
          'the dictionary of names, the definitions [1A], [1B] and the four corrections.',
          '**2. Recap of 4+4.nb** — $\\sigma$, $s_h$, $t_h$, $\\zeta_A$, $\\tau_A$, $\\bar\\tau_A$, $T_A$, $P_L$, $P_R$, $u$, $E_{44}$, the split '
          'table `gridx44` compared with the table printed by 4+4.nb, split product, zero divisors.',
          '**3. The octonions** — $\\tau^{O}$, vielbein $E^{O}$, `gridO`, Fano plane (with a picture), the laws of [1A], the '
          'eight-square identity, non-associativity, a numerical example, Cayley–Dickson, comparison with the split octonions.',
          '**4. Clifford algebras** — $T^{O}$ ($Cl(8,0)$), $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}$ (real 7+1), $\\Gamma^{(7,1)}$ (complexified 7+1), the theorem, '
          '$\\widehat\\Gamma^{(7,1)}$ (real 32×32) and its quaternionic commutant, chirality, the octonionic meaning.',
          '**5. Examples** — so(1,7), boost and rotation, Dirac operator, real on-shell spinor amplitudes, real massless (16 '
          'components) and massive (32 components) spinor fields, all nine signatures.',
          '**6. Summary of all checks** of sections 1–5 — `Length[checks]`, `failedChecks`, `allChecksPassed` and a table of the checks.',
          '**7. The Dirac equation of the quantized 16-component field dirac16complex** — real Dirac matrices, Dirac and Klein–Gordon '
          'equations, Hamiltonian, real spinors, spin sums, Grassmann algebra, quantization, charge conjugation, Lorentz covariance, '
          'chirality and the two coupled 8-component equations (section 11.1–11.2).',
          '**8. A 16-component Kohn–Sham theory of the self-coupled field** — Lagrangian, nonlinear field equation, Hohenberg–Kohn, '
          'Kohn–Sham equations, exchange (Wick), Fierz, uniform matter (section 11.3, 11.5).',
          '**9. The chiral Kohn–Sham theory (8 + 8 components)** — chiral densities, chiral Kohn–Sham equations, chiral symmetry, the '
          'chiral vacuum with the Dirac sea (section 11.4–11.5).',
          '**10. Summary of all checks** of sections 1–9.'] + (S3.notebook_items(D['s3']['n_last']) if D.get('s3') else []))
    d.p('To re-evaluate: open 7+1.nb in Wolfram 15.0.1, start from a fresh kernel (Evaluation ▸ Quit Kernel ▸ Local, if the kernel has '
        'been used: the tensor packages xAct of section 12 keep their definitions in the kernel) and choose Evaluation ▸ Evaluate Notebook '
        '%s; or run `Get["D:/Developer/github/7+1/tools/run_eval.wl"]` from any notebook, which evaluates 7+1.nb in place '
        'in a fresh kernel (a front-end kernel configuration "Eval71" that it adds for the purpose and removes again) and saves it. '
        'The notebook is generated by `tools/make_7p1_nb.py` (`python3 tools/make_7p1_nb.py` writes the unevaluated 7+1.nb).'
        % ('(about %.0f seconds)' % float(log['seconds']) if log.get('seconds') else '(the duration is recorded in build/eval_log.txt)'))

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    from doc_dirac16 import num as num16
    hs, nj = D['hs'], D['nj']
    d.h1('11. Sections 7–10: the Dirac equation and the Kohn–Sham theories of dirac16complex')
    d.p('Sections 7–10 of 7+1.nb were appended in a second stage (sections 0–6 are unchanged). They contain the Dirac equation of a '
        '16-component quantized fermion field named dirac16complex on the real 7+1 spacetime, with eight REAL 16×16 Dirac matrices; '
        'its chiral form as two 8-component equations; and the 16-component and chiral Kohn–Sham theories of that field coupled to '
        'itself. This section states the results with the notation of this document (section 3); %d checks of 7+1.nb verify them.'
        % len(D['checks79']))
    d.h2('11.1 The Dirac equation with real Dirac matrices')
    d.p('The field is the column $\\Psi=(\\Psi_1,\\dots,\\Psi_{16})^{\\mathsf T}$ (`dirac16complex`) of complex anticommuting (Grassmann-odd) '
        'operators, with the equal-time anticommutators')
    d.eq(r'\Psi_a(x_0,\vec x)\Psi_b(x_0,\vec y)+\Psi_b(x_0,\vec y)\Psi_a(x_0,\vec x)=0,\qquad '
         r'\Psi_a(x_0,\vec x)\Psi_b^\dagger(x_0,\vec y)+\Psi_b^\dagger(x_0,\vec y)\Psi_a(x_0,\vec x)=\delta_{ab}\,\delta^7(\vec x-\vec y),')
    d.p('where $\\vec x=(x_1,\\dots,x_7)$ here denotes the space coordinates. The Dirac matrices are the real generators of section 7.2, '
        '$\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_0=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1_8\\\\1_8&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, '
        '$\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_i=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}\\tau^{O}_i&0\\\\0&-\\tau^{O}_i\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, with '
        '$\\Gamma^{A}=\\eta_{(1,7)}^{AA}\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$ and $\\bar\\Psi=\\Psi^\\dagger\\Gamma^{0}$:')
    d.align([r'&\big(i\,\Gamma^{A}\partial_A-m\big)\Psi=0,\qquad \big(i\Gamma^{A}\partial_A+m\big)\big(i\Gamma^{A}\partial_A-m\big)=-\big(\partial_0^2-\partial_1^2-\dots-\partial_7^2+m^2\big)1_{16},',
             r'&i\,\partial_0\Psi=H_{\rm D}\Psi,\qquad H_{\rm D}=-i\,\Gamma^{0}\Gamma^{i}\partial_i+m\,\Gamma^{0},\qquad '
             r'\partial_A\big(\bar\Psi\Gamma^{A}\Psi\big)=0 .'])
    d.p('$\\Gamma^0$ and $\\Gamma^0\\Gamma^i$ are real symmetric, so $H_{\\rm D}$ is Hermitian. The factor $i$ is required: with real matrices, '
        '$(\\Gamma^A\\partial_A)^2=\\partial_0^2-\\partial_1^2-\\dots-\\partial_7^2$, and only $i\\Gamma^A\\partial_A$ gives the Klein–Gordon operator '
        'with $+m^2$. For a plane wave $e^{-i(Ex_0-\\vec p\\cdot\\vec x)}$, $H_{\\rm D}$ becomes the real matrix '
        '$\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&m1_8+p^i\\tau^{O}_i\\\\m1_8-p^i\\tau^{O}_i&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, and since '
        '$(m1_8+p^i\\tau^{O}_i)(m1_8-p^i\\tau^{O}_i)=E^21_8$, $E=\\sqrt{m^2+\\vec p^{\\,2}}$, its positive-energy eigenvectors are REAL: '
        '$w_s=\\frac1{\\sqrt E}\\big(E\\,e_s;\\ (m1_8-p^i\\tau^{O}_i)e_s\\big)$, $s=1,\\dots,8$ ($e_s$ the unit columns of length 8), real '
        'amplitudes with $(\\not p-m)w_s=0$ as in section 8.4. The notebook also verifies the spin sums, the mode expansion with the canonical '
        'anticommutators, charge conjugation $\\Psi^c=\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8\\Psi^*$ and Lorentz covariance.')
    d.h2('11.2 Chiral projections: two coupled 8-component equations')
    d.p('There is no real chirality matrix (section 8.5: the real matrices anticommuting with all $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$ are the multiples of '
        '$\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8$, which squares to $-1_{16}$). With the complex chirality $\\chi^{(1,7)}=i\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8$ (section 7.6) the chiral '
        'projections $P^{(1,7)}_{L,R}=\\frac12\\big(1_{16}\\mp\\chi^{(1,7)}\\big)$ give, with $\\Psi=(\\Psi_\\uparrow;\\Psi_\\downarrow)$ (upper and lower 8 '
        'components),')
    d.align([r'\Psi_L&=\frac{\Psi_\uparrow+i\,\Psi_\downarrow}{\sqrt2},\qquad \Psi_R=\frac{i\,\Psi_\uparrow+\Psi_\downarrow}{\sqrt2},',
             r'P^{(1,7)}_L\Psi&=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1_8\\-i1_8\end{pmatrix}\Psi_L,\qquad P^{(1,7)}_R\Psi=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}-i1_8\\1_8\end{pmatrix}\Psi_R,',
             r'\big(i\,\partial_0+\tau^{O}_i\,\partial_i\big)\Psi_L&=m\,\Psi_R,\qquad \big(i\,\partial_0-\tau^{O}_i\,\partial_i\big)\Psi_R=m\,\Psi_L .'])
    d.p('The two 8-component equations contain only the real matrices $\\tau^{O}_i$; the mass couples them, and for $m=0$ they decouple.')
    d.h2('11.3 The self-coupled field and its 16-component Kohn–Sham theory')
    d.p('With $\\chi=\\chi^{(1,7)}$, four real couplings $g_S,g_P,g_V,g_A$ and four real external sources $\\mathsf s,\\mathsf p,\\mathsf v_A,\\mathsf a_A$:')
    d.align([r'\mathcal L&=\bar\Psi\big(i\Gamma^{A}\partial_A-m\big)\Psi-\bar\Psi\big(\mathsf s+i\chi\,\mathsf p+\Gamma^{A}(\mathsf v_A+\chi\,\mathsf a_A)\big)\Psi',
             r'&\quad-\frac{g_S}2(\bar\Psi\Psi)^2-\frac{g_P}2(\bar\Psi i\chi\Psi)^2-\frac{g_V}2(\bar\Psi\Gamma^{A}\Psi)(\bar\Psi\Gamma_{A}\Psi)-\frac{g_A}2(\bar\Psi\Gamma^{A}\chi\Psi)(\bar\Psi\Gamma_{A}\chi\Psi),'])
    d.p('where $\\Gamma_A=\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$. The densities $\\rho_S=\\bar\\Psi\\Psi$, $\\rho_P=\\bar\\Psi i\\chi\\Psi$, $j^A=\\bar\\Psi\\Gamma^A\\Psi$, '
        '$j_5^A=\\bar\\Psi\\Gamma^A\\chi\\Psi$ (18 real fields) determine the ground state (Hohenberg–Kohn), and the Kohn–Sham orbitals $\\Phi_k$ obey')
    d.align([r'H_{\rm KS}\Phi_k&=\lambda_k\Phi_k,',
             r'H_{\rm KS}&=\Gamma^{0}\Gamma^{i}\big(-i\partial_i+\mathcal V_i\big)+\Gamma^{0}\big(m+\mathcal S\big)+\mathcal V_0+\Gamma^{0}i\chi\,\mathcal P+\chi\,\mathcal A_0+\Gamma^{0}\Gamma^{i}\chi\,\mathcal A_i,',
             r'\mathcal S&=\mathsf s+g_S\rho_S+\frac{\delta E_{xc}}{\delta\rho_S},\qquad \mathcal P=\mathsf p+g_P\rho_P+\frac{\delta E_{xc}}{\delta\rho_P},',
             r'\mathcal V_A&=\mathsf v_A+g_Vj_A+\frac{\delta E_{xc}}{\delta j^A},\qquad \mathcal A_A=\mathsf a_A+g_Aj_{5A}+\frac{\delta E_{xc}}{\delta j_5^A},',
             r'\rho_S&=\sum_k\bar\Phi_k\Phi_k,\quad \rho_P=\sum_k\bar\Phi_ki\chi\Phi_k,\quad j^A=\sum_k\bar\Phi_k\Gamma^{A}\Phi_k,\quad j_5^A=\sum_k\bar\Phi_k\Gamma^{A}\chi\Phi_k,'])
    d.p('with $j_A=\\eta^{(1,7)}_{AA}j^A$, the sums running over the occupied positive-energy orbitals (no Dirac sea), and '
        '$E_{xc}=F-T_s-E_H$, where $F$ is the universal functional, $T_s=\\sum_k\\int\\Phi_k^\\dagger H_{\\rm D}\\Phi_k$ and '
        '$E_H=\\int\\big[\\frac{g_S}2\\rho_S^2+\\frac{g_P}2\\rho_P^2+\\frac{g_V}2j^Aj_A+\\frac{g_A}2j_5^Aj_{5A}\\big]$. The exact exchange energy of the '
        'contact couplings follows from Wick’s theorem: with the 16×16 matrix $\\mathcal R=\\sum_k\\Phi_k\\bar\\Phi_k$,')
    d.eq(r'E_x=-\frac{g_S}2\mathrm{tr}(\mathcal R\mathcal R)-\frac{g_P}2\mathrm{tr}(i\chi\mathcal R\,i\chi\mathcal R)'
         r'-\frac{g_V}2\mathrm{tr}(\Gamma^{A}\mathcal R\Gamma_{A}\mathcal R)-\frac{g_A}2\mathrm{tr}(\Gamma^{A}\chi\mathcal R\Gamma_{A}\chi\mathcal R).')
    d.h2('11.4 The chiral Kohn–Sham theory')
    d.p('In chiral components $\\Phi_{kL}$, $\\Phi_{kR}$ (as for $\\Psi_L$, $\\Psi_R$), with $\\kappa=\\sum_k\\Phi_{kL}^\\dagger\\Phi_{kR}$ '
        '($\\rho_S=\\kappa+\\kappa^*$, $\\rho_P=i(\\kappa-\\kappa^*)$), the complex Kohn–Sham mass $\\mathcal M=m+\\mathcal S+i\\mathcal P$ and '
        '$\\mathcal V^{L,R}_A=\\mathcal V_A\\mp\\mathcal A_A$, the Kohn–Sham equation becomes')
    d.align([r'\big[\mathcal V^L_0-\tau^{O}_i\partial_i-i\tau^{O}_i\mathcal V^L_i\big]\Phi_L+\mathcal M\,\Phi_R&=\lambda\,\Phi_L,',
             r'\mathcal M^*\,\Phi_L+\big[\mathcal V^R_0+\tau^{O}_i\partial_i+i\tau^{O}_i\mathcal V^R_i\big]\Phi_R&=\lambda\,\Phi_R .'])
    d.p('The scalar–pseudoscalar Hartree energy $\\frac{g_S-g_P}2(\\kappa^2+\\kappa^{*2})+(g_S+g_P)\\kappa\\kappa^*$ is invariant under the real '
        'chiral rotations $e^{i\\vartheta\\chi}=\\cos\\vartheta\\,1_{16}-\\sin\\vartheta\\,\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_8$ ($\\kappa\\to e^{2i\\vartheta}\\kappa$) exactly when $g_P=g_S$.')
    d.h2('11.5 Examples and verification')
    d.p('**Uniform matter** (units $m=1$, no sea, $g_S=-10^5$, $g_V=10^4$, $g_P=g_A=0$): the Hartree gap equation $m^*=m+g_S\\rho_S(m^*)$ and '
        'the energy density give saturation at the Fermi momentum $k_F=%s$, where the energy per particle is $E/A-m=%s$ (bound) and $m^*=%s$; '
        'there $E/A$ equals the Fermi energy $E_F=g_V\\rho+\\sqrt{k_F^2+m^{*2}}$ (Hugenholtz–Van Hove), with $\\rho=k_F^7/(105\\pi^4)$.'
        % (num16(hs['kF0'], 12), num16(hs['E/A - m'], 12), num16(hs['mstar'], 12)))
    d.p('**The chiral vacuum with the Dirac sea** (all negative-energy orbitals filled, momentum cutoff $|\\vec p|<\\Lambda_{\\rm c}=1$, '
        '$g_S=g_P=g$): for $m=0$ a non-zero mass $|\\mathcal M|$ appears exactly when $g<g_c=-90\\pi^4/\\Lambda_{\\rm c}^6$; for $g=\\frac65g_c$, '
        '$|\\mathcal M|=%s$ — spontaneous breaking of the chiral symmetry.' % num16(nj['|M| (m = 0)'], 12))
    d.p('**Checks.** Sections 7–9 of 7+1.nb contain %d checks, all True (section 9); the independent program `tools/ref_dirac16.py` '
        'repeats the constructions with %d checks, all True, and reproduces every printed number to at least 17 digits.'
        % (len(D['checks79']), len(D['ref_bool'])))

    if D.get('s3'):
        S3.readme_section(d, D['s3'], 12)
    nf = 13 if D.get('s3') else 12
    d.h1('%d. Files in this repository' % nf)
    d.table(['path', 'content'], [
        ['`7+1.nb`', 'the new notebook, evaluated, with all outputs'],
        ['`4+4.nb`', 'the 4+4 notebook (split octonions, $T_A$), the starting point; unchanged'],
        ['`NASH_Mathematica_Notebooks/`', 'earlier notebooks of the project; unchanged'],
        ['`tools/make_7p1_nb.py`, `tools/nbwrite.py`', 'generator of 7+1.nb'],
        ['`tools/run_eval.wl`', 'evaluates 7+1.nb in place in a fresh kernel of Wolfram 15.0.1 and saves it'],
        ['`tools/watch_requests.wl`, `tools/git_push.wl`', 'runs Wolfram 15.0.1 jobs requested in build/request.txt (evaluate, push) without keyboard input; pushes with the local Git'],
        ['`tools/eval_cells_log.wl`', 'development check: evaluates a generated notebook cell by cell and logs messages and checks'],
        ['`tools/ref_dirac16.py`', 'independent Python checks of sections 7–9 (dirac16complex, Kohn–Sham, chiral Kohn–Sham)'],
        ['`tools/ref44.py`, `tools/ref71.py`, `tools/extra71.py`, `tools/octalg.py`', 'independent Python (sympy) implementation and checks'],
        ['`tools/classify_clifford.py`', 'numerical classification of the nine signatures via commutants of realified representations'],
        ['`tools/cayley_dickson_formulas.py`, `tools/cayley_dickson_match.py`', 'tests of Cayley–Dickson formulas against the tables'],
        ['`tools/nbparse.py`, `tools/verify_7p1_nb.py`', 'parser for .nb files; cross-check of the evaluated 7+1.nb'],
        ['`tools/docgen.py`, `tools/doc_*.py`, `tools/make_docs.py`', 'generators of the documentation (Markdown, LaTeX, PDF)'],
    ] + (S3.file_rows() if D.get('s3') else []) + [
    ], widths=[0.45, 0.49])

    d.h1('%d. References' % (nf + 1))
    d.ul(['J. C. Baez, *The Octonions*, Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 39 (2002) 145–205, arXiv:math/0105155 — section 2.2 gives the '
          'Cayley–Dickson product $(a,b)(c,d)=(ac-d\\bar b,\\ \\bar a d+cb)$ used in section 6.5.',
          'H. B. Lawson and M.-L. Michelsohn, *Spin Geometry*, Princeton University Press (1989) — classification of real '
          'Clifford algebras and the periodicity $Cl(p+1,q+1)\\cong Cl(p,q)\\otimes\\mathbb R(2)$.',
          'F. Reese Harvey, *Spinors and Calibrations*, Academic Press (1990) — octonions, triality and spinors.',
          'P. A. M. Dirac, Proc. R. Soc. Lond. A 117 (1928) 610 — the Dirac equation $(i\\gamma^\\mu\\partial_\\mu-m)\\psi=0$, '
          'written in section 11 with the eight real $\\Gamma^{(1,7)}_A$.',
          'P. Hohenberg and W. Kohn, Phys. Rev. 136 (1964) B864; W. Kohn and L. J. Sham, Phys. Rev. 140 (1965) A1133 — the density '
          'determines the ground state; non-interacting Kohn–Sham orbitals reproduce the density (section 11.3).',
          'A. K. Rajagopal and J. Callaway, Phys. Rev. B 7 (1973) 1912; R. M. Dreizler and E. K. U. Gross, *Density Functional Theory*, '
          'Springer (1990) — relativistic density-functional theory, the no-sea approximation.',
          'G. C. Wick, Phys. Rev. 80 (1950) 268; M. Fierz, Z. Phys. 104 (1937) 553 — the exchange energy of contact couplings.',
          'J. D. Walecka, Ann. Phys. 83 (1974) 491; N. M. Hugenholtz and L. Van Hove, Physica 24 (1958) 363 — saturation of uniform '
          'matter, and $E/A=E_F$ at saturation (section 11.5).',
          'Y. Nambu and G. Jona-Lasinio, Phys. Rev. 122 (1961) 345 — spontaneous chiral symmetry breaking by contact couplings with a cutoff '
          '(section 11.5).'] + (S3.references() if D.get('s3') else []))
    return d
